# The deployed index.html is LF. This machine has core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites the
# working tree to CRLF on checkout and silently adds one byte per line (+3,887 bytes on
# index.html alone). That breaks the byte-for-byte parity check the EPIC-1 refactor is
# measured against, so line endings are pinned here rather than left to local git config.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.ttf binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary
*.pdf binary
*.docx binary

# ---- Gradle wrapper (EPIC-6) ----
# The blanket `* text=auto eol=lf` above is right for this repo's web sources and wrong for
# the wrapper Capacitor generates:
#
#   *.jar    survives today only because text=auto sniffs NUL bytes and opts out. That is a
#            heuristic, not a guarantee; a jar normalised as text is silently corrupted and
#            the failure is an unreadable stack trace at build time. Made explicit.
#   *.bat    cmd.exe is historically unreliable with LF-only batch files, particularly
#            around labels and goto. Windows batch wants CRLF.
#   gradlew  the POSIX shell script must stay LF whatever the platform.
*.jar binary
*.bat text eol=crlf
gradlew text eol=lf
